+## Current activation (cost-sensitivity of momentum + signal-defect repair — 45085425994, campaign slot 02, task R-001)
+
+Task R-001 asked whether the short-horizon momentum edge survives conservative
+transaction-cost stress on the collected universe. Executing it exposed a
+**defect in the momentum signal itself**, which changes the interpretation of
+every earlier momentum result. Both findings are recorded here.
+
+### Finding 1 (blocking): `momentum_signals` was not a momentum signal
+
+`research.backtest.regime_stability.momentum_signals` evaluated
+
+    ret = mean(log(closes[i - lookback + 1 : i + 1]))
+
+That expression is the **mean log price level** over the trailing window, not a
+return. Its sign compares the window's mean price against a fixed zero level,
+so on any price series that stays far above zero it is positive on essentially
+every bar. Measured on the collected universe (campaign slot 02):
+
+* position flips across 4465 bars: **1** on nine of ten assets (NVDA: 2);
+* long fraction: 0.998-0.999 on nine of ten assets (NVDA: 0.584);
+* the zero-cost medians in `state/check_artifacts/momentum_results.json` are
+  reproduced **exactly** by an explicit always-long (+1 every bar) benchmark on
+  nine of ten assets (all except NVDA, the one asset with meaningful short
+  exposure).
+
+The signal was therefore an **almost-always-long buy-and-hold bet**, not a
+long/short momentum rule. This also explains why transaction costs had almost
+no effect in the first (pre-fix) cost run: a buy-and-hold position trades
+essentially once, so a per-turn cost model has almost nothing to charge. The
+cost grid was, in effect, not stress-testing the signal.
+
+The function is repaired to the past-only return
+
+    ret = closes[i] / closes[i - lookback] - 1
+
+Signal at bar `i` uses `closes[:i+1]` only; bar `i`'s close is the fill price
+for the position held into bar `i+1`. Post-fix flip counts on AMZN are
+1217 / 907 / 599 for lookbacks 3 / 5 / 10, and the signal is exactly
+`sign(closes[i]/closes[i-lookback] - 1)` on every bar.
+
+**This repair retracts, rather than confirms, the earlier momentum
+admission.** The following durable conclusions are withdrawn:
+
+| Prior record | Status after repair |
+|---|---|
+| Momentum SUPPORTED, REGIME_STABLE positive in 7/10 assets (37318950814) | **RETRACTED** — the medians are a buy-and-hold benchmark, not a momentum edge |
+| Momentum lookback-robust 3/5/10, 6/10 ROBUST (37361000967) | **RETRACTED** — the sweep varied a window mean that is ~constant, so the lookbacks were near-identical by construction |
+| Momentum horizon-stable in magnitude at 20/60 (37394344201) | **RETRACTED** for the same reason |
+
+`research/checks/verify_momentum.py`,
+`verify_momentum_longer_horizon.py`, and
+`verify_momentum_lookback_sweep.py` each re-implemented the *same* mean-log
+formula, so their MATCH verdicts confirmed that the check and the verifier
+agreed — not that the signal was momentum. Agreement between two paths that
+share a conceptual defect is not independent validation of the concept. The
+verifier pattern is sound; its blind spot is that all paths encoded the same
+specification.
+
+### Finding 2 (task answer): corrected momentum shows no positive edge at any cost point
+
+Cost grid, declared before execution and not adjusted afterwards:
+
+| cost point | commission/trade | commission/share | slippage (cents) | slippage (proportional) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `zero` | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
+| `realistic` | 2.0 | 0.003 | 2.0 | 0.0005 |
+| `stress2x` | 4.0 | 0.006 | 4.0 | 0.0010 |
+
+`realistic` is the repository's established realistic bundle
+(`examples/ma_crossover.py`, `ma_crossover_real_data.py`,
+`volatility_regime_filter.py`); `stress2x` doubles it.
+
+Corrected momentum, lookbacks 3 / 5 / 10, 10 assets, walk-forward
+train=252/test=84/warmup=60/overlap=60, 4 contiguous volatility blocks, matched
+coin-flip null **under the same cost config**:
+
+* **zero-cost EDGE assets at lookback 5: 0/10.** No asset has uniformly
+  positive segment medians with a REGIME_STABLE verdict.
+* **realistic-cost EDGE assets at lookback 5: 0/10.**
+* **Cost-robustness classes over all 30 (asset, lookback) cells:
+  COST_ROBUST=0, COST_FRAGILE=0, COST_REVERSED=0, NO_EDGE_ZERO=30.**
+  The classification is degenerate because nothing was an edge at zero cost:
+  the cost question does not even arise for a signal with no edge.
+* Verdict counts (lookback 5): CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE=6, REGIME_STABLE=3,
+  REGIME_DEPENDENT=1. Of the three REGIME_STABLE cases (AAPL, META, NVDA),
+  AAPL and NVDA each have a negative segment median, and META is uniformly
+  negative (`[-0.129, -0.041, +0.047]`) — these are stability of *mixed or
+  negative* outcomes, not a positive edge. The framework's REGIME_STABLE
+  verdict describes cross-segment dispersion, not sign.
+
+Candidate-minus-null gaps at lookback 5 are small and inconsistent in sign
+across assets, and the corrected medians are negative in most segments of
+most assets. Magnitudes are unchanged in order between cost points (a few
+hundredths of a log return per 84-bar fold), which is expected: at these fold
+lengths even 2x costs are a modest per-fold drag. The decisive change is
+between the **defective** signal (0/10 with a large positive buy-and-hold
+median) and the **corrected** signal (0/10 with no positive edge at all).
+
+### Verification
+
+* `research/checks/momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` — exits 0; manifest 10/10
+  OK; PREFLIGHT PASSED; leakage PASS on AMZN/JPM (signals + equity-fill
+  audit); 90 (asset, lookback, cost) cells written; determinism r1==r2.
+* `research/checks/verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` — independent code
+  path (own CSV reader, own signal formula, own segment reconstruction,
+  `walk_forward` called directly, `noise_benchmark` for the null):
+  Gate 1 signal contract PASS (AMZN/JPM, flips 1217/907/599 and 1199/887/621,
+  formula exact); Gate 2 all 90 cells MATCH; Gate 3 shows 9/10 pre-correction
+  artifacts equal an explicit always-long benchmark; Gate 4 determinism
+  identical.
+* `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` — **174 tests, all passing**
+  (168 existing + 6 new `TestMomentumSignal` regression guards that pin the
+  signal to `sign(closes[i]/closes[i-lookback]-1)`, require frequent flips on
+  a realistic-priced series, and require exact agreement with an independent
+  return definition).
+
+### Interpretation and scope of the retraction
+
+The candidate positive momentum evidence is **withdrawn**. The earlier
+"momentum" verdicts described an almost-always-long equity position; the
+genuine short-horizon long/short momentum rule shows no positive REGIME_STABLE
+edge at lookbacks 3/5/10 on this universe, at zero cost or under conservative
+costs.
+
+Cross-sectional momentum
+(`research/checks/cross_sectional_momentum.py`) and the breakout class
+(`breakout_20day_cont.py`) do **not** call `momentum_signals` — they compute
+their own past-only rank and threshold rules — so their FALSIFIED verdicts are
+unaffected by this repair. The affected artifacts are
+`momentum_results.json`, `momentum_lookback_sweep_results.json`,
+`momentum_longer_horizon_results.json`, `momentum_sweep.py`,
+`momentum_hold_period_sensitivity.py`, and their three verifiers, all of which
+encode the pre-repair formula and must be regenerated before any of their
+numbers are reused.
+
+The methodological lesson is recorded in `REPOSITORY_PLAYBOOK.md`: a verifier
+that re-implements the same specification is a determinism and arithmetic
+check, not an independent test of the specification. Independently checking the
+*specification's consequences* (here: flip frequency and an explicit
+always-long benchmark) is what exposed the defect.
+
+---
+
